From ee6d8c65911e80b501d0f92b820d519ba5c03643 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Frdlik <michaelf.ms@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 31 Jul 2026 07:41:46 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 10/14] Thylacine CL-7b-2: assert the unwinder instead of
 probing for it

Adding __register_frame to the process-symbols table did not fix the device
failure, and finding out why corrected the mechanism: that lookup never goes
through the JITDylib at all.

config-ix.cmake settles HAVE_(DE)REGISTER_FRAME with check_symbol_exists,
which compiles AND LINKS a probe program. The aarch64-thylacine cross
configure has no libunwind on its link line, so the probe fails and LLVM
concludes the platform has no unwinder. Both consumers then take their #else
arm and resolve __register_frame through
DynamicLibrary::SearchForAddressOfSymbol -- a lookup that a statically linked
binary with no loader can never satisfy, no matter what the binary defines.
The error message only exists in that arm, so seeing it on-device is itself
proof the defines were absent.

The probe's ANSWER is wrong, not its question: libunwind.a supplies both
symbols and every Thylacine binary reaching this code links the C++ runtime
that carries them (llvm-nm on the built osmesa-prove: both defined T). So
assert it.

Asserting in the two source files rather than in the CMake cache is
deliberate. config.h is included nearly everywhere, so answering it there
regenerates the header and rebuilds the whole cross tree for a fact that two
translation units consume. Keeping it beside the code that depends on it also
survives a build configured some other way. The #ifndef guards mean a future
toolchain whose probe succeeds simply wins. If the assertion ever becomes
untrue the result is a loud undefined-symbol link error, not a silent
runtime fallback -- the failure mode moves from invisible to unmissable.

RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp is not on the CL-7 path (gallivm reaches ORC/JITLink)
but carries the identical defect, so it gets the identical fix. A latent bug
is still a bug.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01RXubKBEPsQ7gdi7N6wG5mK
---
 .../Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp    | 29 +++++++++++++++++++
 .../RuntimeDyld/RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp       | 19 ++++++++++++
 2 files changed, 48 insertions(+)

diff --git a/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp b/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp
index 1856181e3..d539bfdb2 100644
--- a/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp
+++ b/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp
@@ -13,6 +13,35 @@
 #include "llvm/Support/DynamicLibrary.h"
 #include "llvm/Support/raw_ostream.h"
 
+// Thylacine (CL-7b-2): assert the unwinder rather than probing for it.
+//
+// config-ix.cmake settles HAVE_(DE)REGISTER_FRAME with check_symbol_exists,
+// which COMPILES AND LINKS a probe. A cross configure for aarch64-thylacine has
+// no libunwind on its link line, so the probe fails and LLVM concludes the
+// platform has no unwinder -- taking the fallback arm below, which resolves
+// __register_frame through DynamicLibrary::SearchForAddressOfSymbol. Thylacine
+// binaries are statically linked and there is no loader, so that lookup can
+// NEVER succeed: measured on-device as "could not register eh-frame:
+// __register_frame function not found", with every JIT materialization failing
+// behind it, so llvmpipe could not compile a single shader.
+//
+// The probe's answer is wrong, not its question. libunwind.a supplies both
+// symbols, and every Thylacine binary that reaches this code links the C++
+// runtime that carries them (verified with llvm-nm on the built osmesa-prove:
+// both defined T). Asserting it here rather than in the CMake cache keeps the
+// fact next to the code that depends on it, costs one translation unit to
+// rebuild instead of regenerating config.h, and cannot be lost by a build that
+// configures LLVM some other way. If it ever becomes untrue the result is a
+// loud undefined-symbol link error, not a silent runtime fallback.
+#if defined(__thylacine__)
+#ifndef HAVE_REGISTER_FRAME
+#define HAVE_REGISTER_FRAME 1
+#endif
+#ifndef HAVE_DEREGISTER_FRAME
+#define HAVE_DEREGISTER_FRAME 1
+#endif
+#endif // __thylacine__
+
 #define DEBUG_TYPE "orc"
 
 using namespace llvm;
diff --git a/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/RuntimeDyld/RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp b/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/RuntimeDyld/RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp
index 10160ddbf..0b534a657 100644
--- a/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/RuntimeDyld/RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp
+++ b/llvm/lib/ExecutionEngine/RuntimeDyld/RTDyldMemoryManager.cpp
@@ -25,6 +25,25 @@
 #include <unistd.h>
 #endif
 
+// Thylacine (CL-7b-2): the same assertion as
+// lib/ExecutionEngine/Orc/TargetProcess/RegisterEHFrames.cpp -- see the long
+// comment there for why config-ix.cmake's check_symbol_exists probe answers
+// wrongly on a cross build, and why a SearchForAddressOfSymbol fallback can
+// never work on a statically linked system with no loader.
+//
+// Mesa's gallivm reaches the ORC/JITLink path, not this one, so this file is
+// not on the CL-7 critical path. It carries the identical defect, though, and a
+// latent one is still one -- anything that does take the RTDyld path would fail
+// the same way for the same reason.
+#if defined(__thylacine__)
+#ifndef HAVE_REGISTER_FRAME
+#define HAVE_REGISTER_FRAME 1
+#endif
+#ifndef HAVE_DEREGISTER_FRAME
+#define HAVE_DEREGISTER_FRAME 1
+#endif
+#endif // __thylacine__
+
 namespace llvm {
 
 RTDyldMemoryManager::~RTDyldMemoryManager() = default;
